WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 45: The Orphan Trains and the Children of the Prairie

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.600
[Rebecca] In the mid-19th century, the crowded streets of New York City were filled with tragedy.

4
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:28.960
[Rebecca] As tens of thousands of impoverished European immigrants flooded into lower Manhattan slums, disease, industrial accidents, and malnutrition left an estimated 30,000 children homeless, roaming the docks, sleeping in cardboard alleys, and selling matches to survive.

5
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:37.680
[Rebecca] In 1853, a compassionate reforming young minister named Charles Loring Brace devised an audacious, controversial social experiment.

6
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:44.560
[Rebecca] Load the children onto steam trains and send them thousands of miles west to the rolling farm country of the Midwest.

7
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:54.720
[Rebecca] For 75 years, the orphan trains rolled across Nebraska, Kansas, and Iowa, carrying more than a quarter of a million children toward an unknown destiny.

8
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.440
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

9
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:19.120
[George] In notorious slums like the Five Points, immigrant families from Ireland, Germany, and Italy lived crammed into unventilated tenements with open sewers.

10
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:28.480
[George] Epidemics of cholera, typhoid, and tuberculosis ravaged the population, killing thousands of parents and leaving children orphaned on the streets.

11
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:44.800
[George] Contemporaries referred to these homeless youths as street Arabs, ragged, barefoot boys and girls as young as five years old who slept in alleyways, huddling over iron steam grates in winter and earning pennies as newswise, shoe shiners or flower peddlers.

12
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:53.440
[George] Police viewed them as an emerging criminal underclass, frequently arresting children and locking them in adult penitentiaries on Blackwell's Island.

13
00:01:53.440 --> 00:02:02.760
[George] Witnessing this horrifying crisis, a 27-year-old Yale-educated congregationalist minister named Charles Loring Brace resolved to intervene.

14
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:07.080
[George] In 1853, Braunded the Children's Aid Society.

15
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:21.720
[George] Believing that the only way to save these children from moral and physical destruction was to remove them entirely from the corrupting city and place them into the wholesome, hard-working, agricultural embrace of American farm families in the West.

16
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:33.240
[George] Thousands of immigrant children lived on the frozen cobblestones of Five Points, sleeping in cellar-grating vents and selling newspapers for pennies while disease and starvation swept the tenements.

17
00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:44.120
[George] Police sweeps routinely cleared homeless children from market awnings and coal cellars, funneling them into overcrowded city asylums where mortality rates were devastatingly high.

18
00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:51.880
[George] Brace's revolutionary system was christened the emigration plan, but the public soon knew it simply as the orphan trains.

19
00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:56.600
[George] Working with local churches, charitable committees and rural newspapers.

20
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:02.840
[George] The Children's Aid Society identified Midwestern farming communities willing to receive children.

21
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:15.800
[George] Before departure, children were scrubbed clean, fitted with a single new set of sturdy clothes, given a Bible, and escorted aboard chartered railroad cars by female agents who served as chaperones.

22
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:26.040
[George] For children who had never seen anything beyond dark brick tenements and cobblestone alleys, the cross-country train journey was an overwhelming, mind-altering revelation.

23
00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:43.880
[George] As the steam locomotives crossed the Mississippi and Missouri Rivers into Nebraska and Iowa, children pressed their faces against the glass, gazing out in stunned disbelief at endless, rolling oceans of green prairie grass, blue skies that stretched forever, and grazing cattle herds.

24
00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:54.720
[George] The train rides lasted for days, filled with nervous excitement, homesickness, and quiet tears as children wondered what kind of strangers awaited them at the end of the line.

25
00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:06.800
[George] Charles Loring Brace believed that the Christian agrarian farm home was the true moral antidote to urban degradation, organizing chaperoned passenger coaches equipped with clean clothes and warm food.

26
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:16.800
[George] Traveling agents accompanied every train, checking on the welfare of the children and interviewing prospective adoptive parents before any indenture agreements were signed.

27
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:31.360
[George] Upon reaching a designated depot town, such as Grand Island, Fremont or Kearney, Nebraska, the train cars emptied, and the children were marched in a single-file procession to the local town hall, church, or opera house.

28
00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:40.400
[George] Advance handbills had been posted in local post offices and general stores announcing a company of orphan children will arrive on Tuesday.

29
00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.000
[George] Farmers and others wanting children, come and see.

30
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:51.600
[George] Inside the crowded hall, the children were placed on an elevated wooden stage before hundreds of inspecting farmers and townspeople.

31
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.000
[George] The selection process was often emotionally grueling and humiliating.

32
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:09.440
[George] Prospective foster parents examined the children like livestock, feeling their biceps, checking their teeth for disease, looking at their hands for calluses, and asking how hard they could work in cornfields or milk cows.

33
00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:20.960
[George] Siblings clung desperately to each other's hands, weeping openly as farmers chose a sturdy twelve-year-old boy for heavy field work while leaving behind a frail younger sister or a toddler.

34
00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:32.920
[George] While agents made earnest attempts to keep brothers and sisters together, hundreds of families were torn apart on the prairie platforms, separated by dozens of miles and lost to each other for decades.

35
00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:45.720
[George] At prairie depots, children were placed upon raised wooden platforms in churches or town halls, where local farmers examined their teeth, muscles and hands to determine their suitability for farm chores.

36
00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:52.600
[George] For the children who stepped off the trains, their new lives on the frontier were as diverse as the human heart itself.

37
00:05:52.600 --> 00:06:01.160
[George] For many, the transition was a miraculous deliverance from starvation and neglect into warmth, education, and boundless familial love.

38
00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:12.360
[George] Thousands of childless pioneer couples welcomed the orphans as their own flesh and blood, legally adopting them, nurturing their talents, and leaving them productive homesteads in their wills.

39
00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:17.320
[George] But for others, the system was a harsh, exploitative form of indentured labor.

40
00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:34.280
[George] Some frontier farmers viewed the orphan boys merely as free hired hands to plow rocky fields, chop timber, and shovel manure from dawn to dusk, treating them with cold emotional indifference, feeding them scraps on back porches, and forbidding them from attending local school.

41
00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:49.800
[George] The Children's Aid Society employed traveling agents to conduct annual inspections, removing children from abusive homes when cruelty was discovered, but across millions of square miles of rural countryside, full oversight was an impossible task.

42
00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:59.960
[George] Children had to rely on their own internal grit, resilience and adaptability to survive the cultural shock of transitioning from Manhattan pavement to prairie soil.

43
00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:11.480
[George] Many children were embraced as beloved daughters and sons, learning to drive horse teams, harvest corn, and attend rural one-room schoolhouses alongside biological siblings.

44
00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:16.240
[George] Brace's Protestant-led organization provoked intense religious controversy.

45
00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:29.760
[George] The Catholic Church in New York grew alarmed that thousands of baptized Irish and Italian Catholic children were being placed into Protestant households across the rural Midwest, where their original faith was often stripped away.

46
00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:36.960
[George] In 1869, the Sisters of Charity founded the New York Foundling Hospital to provide a Catholic alternative.

47
00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:46.240
[George] Led by the remarkable sister Irene Fitzgibbon, the Foundling Hospital refined the orphan train model into a far more structured, compassionate system.

48
00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:53.760
[George] Unlike Brace's public stages, the sisters established strict prearranged placements through local parish priests.

49
00:07:53.760 --> 00:08:05.920
[George] Catholic families in Nebraska and the Midwest had to fill out detailed application forms, providing letters of recommendation from their parish priest regarding their moral character and financial stability.

50
00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:23.760
[George] When the baby trains arrived at Western stations, each infant and toddler wore a numbered brass tag pinned to their woolen dress that matched a corresponding numbered claim ticket held by their pre-approved adoptive parents, ensuring immediate, loving transfers without humiliating public auctions.

51
00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:35.760
[George] The Sisters of Charity of the New York Foundling Hospital created their own Catholic placement system, tagging children with numbers and matching them to pre screened Catholic parish families across the West.

52
00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:42.160
[George] The long term impact of the orphan trains was an extraordinary triumph of human potential over adversity.

53
00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:52.080
[George] Despite their traumatic origins, the vast majority of orphan train riders grew into productive, respected citizens who helped build the modern American Midwest.

54
00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:58.080
[George] They became pioneering farmers, teachers, doctors, merchants, and community leaders.

55
00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.200
[George] Two orphan train riders achieved remarkable political heights.

56
00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:15.160
[George] John Green Brady, an impoverished street waf placed with an Indiana farm family, went on to graduate from Yale and was appointed Territorial Governor of Alaska by President William McKinley, while Andrew H.

57
00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:25.320
[George] Burke, an orphan who arrived in Indiana at age eight, grew up to become a successful banker and was elected governor of North Dakota in 1891.

58
00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:30.840
[George] Other riders became prominent judges, corporate executives, and college professors.

59
00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:43.960
[George] Proving Charles Loring Brace's core belief that poverty at birth does not determine character, and that every child, when provided with opportunity, fresh air, and an honest chance, can rise to greatness.

60
00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:56.520
[George] Orphan train riders rose to extraordinary prominence in American life, including John Green Brady who became territorial governor of Alaska, and Andrew Burke, who was elected governor of North Dakota.

61
00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:08.360
[George] By the late 1920s, changing social philosophies, the rise of modern state foster care systems, and the implementation of child labor laws brought an end to the orphan trains.

62
00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:15.640
[George] The final official train rolled into Sulphur Springs, Texas, on May 31, 1929.

63
00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:28.520
[George] In total, between 1854 and 1929, an estimated 250,000 children had been transported westward, the largest documented child migration in American history.

64
00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:33.800
[George] Today, an estimated two million Americans are descendants of orphan train riders.

65
00:10:33.800 --> 00:10:44.200
[George] In Concordia, Kansas, the National Orphan Train Complex preserves the photographs, train tickets, and oral histories of the brave children who journeyed into the unknown.

66
00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:59.520
[George] The orphan trains remain an extraordinary, complex chapter in the American saga, a mixture of frontier necessity, tragic separation, and profound redemption that proved that a home is not just a place where you are born, but where you are embraced.

67
00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:10.800
[George] Today, an estimated two million Americans are descendants of orphan train riders, honoring an unprecedented migration that founded modern foster care in the United States.

68
00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:32.720
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we explore the bloody clash of ideologies in Bleeding Kansas and John Brown's Raid on Potawatomi.

69
00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:35.120
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

